questions.question:
What does this print, and why is the second line surprising?

const defaults = { theme: "light", nested: { lang: "en" } };
const settings = Object.assign(defaults, { theme: "dark" });
console.log(settings === defaults);
settings.nested.lang = "fr";
console.log(defaults.nested.lang);

questions.starter_code:
const pick = (obj, keys) => {
  // your code
};

console.log(pick({ name: "Kakade", age: 30, city: "Pune" }, ["name", "city", "missing"]));

questions.answer:
const pick = (obj, keys) =>
  Object.fromEntries(
    keys
      .filter((key) => Object.hasOwn(obj, key))
      .map((key) => [key, obj[key]])
  );

console.log(pick({ name: "Kakade", age: 30, city: "Pune" }, ["name", "city", "missing"]));
// { name: 'Kakade', city: 'Pune' }

Object.hasOwn filters out missing keys and inherited ones — using `key in obj` would let prototype properties through. map builds [key, value] pairs, and Object.fromEntries turns them into a fresh object, so the input is untouched. The same idea in reverse gives omit: filter out the listed keys. Note the copy is shallow, which is fine for whitelisting fields but not for editing nested values.

questions.starter_code:
const deepFreeze = (obj) => {
  // your code
};

const config = deepFreeze({ a: 1, nested: { b: 2 } });
config.nested.b = 99;
console.log(config.nested.b, Object.isFrozen(config.nested));

questions.answer:
const deepFreeze = (obj) => {
  Object.values(obj).forEach((value) => {
    if (value && typeof value === "object") deepFreeze(value);
  });
  return Object.freeze(obj);
};

const config = deepFreeze({ a: 1, nested: { b: 2 } });
config.nested.b = 99;
console.log(config.nested.b, Object.isFrozen(config.nested));
// 2 true

Object.freeze is shallow, so the recursion walks each value first and freezes the children before the parent. The `value && typeof value === "object"` guard skips primitives and null (typeof null is "object"). In sloppy mode the later write fails silently; in strict mode or an ES module it throws TypeError. Watch out for cycles — this naive version would recurse forever; a WeakSet of visited objects fixes that.

